;;; `std.algebra/total-owed` — the requirement, **outstanding**.
;;;
;;; The other half of `19_total_violated.ein`, and the two are one property
;;; seen at its two ends. `total` is about a candidate set of size **0** — Ann
;;; has every Food explicitly excluded, so the requirement can never be met and
;;; the state is dead. `total-owed` is about a set that is simply still empty:
;;; nothing says Ann likes anything and nothing says she does not, so the
;;; requirement is **unmet**, not unmeetable.
;;;
;;; `19`'s own comment names this gap — *"it never obliges Ann to like
;;; anything … a state in which nothing at all is known about Ann is not a
;;; violation — it is an open world"* — and says that turning totality into a
;;; first-class obligation is M1d's subject. This file is that subject,
;;; arrived: the same open world, and now the engine can say what it owes in it.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to the rule ─────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two people, two foods, and one arrow:
;;;
;;;   (likes Bob Stew)     stated — Bob's requirement is discharged
;;;   Ann                  nothing at all — one debt
;;;
;;; So `total-owed` fires **once**, for Ann, and does not fire for Bob. That
;;; asymmetry is the whole test: a rule that reported both would be counting
;;; people rather than debts, and one that reported neither would be reading
;;; the guard backwards.
;;;
;;; `24_total_owed_satisfied.ein` is the same file with Ann's arrow added, and
;;; it owes nothing. The pair is this guard's two directions.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation can and cannot observe ────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `:expect` has three forms and all three are assertions about **facts**
;;; (`01_grammar.md` § Query). An `open` conclusion is by construction never a
;;; fact — it is a tally on the search-lattice node, because a stored one would
;;; survive its own discharge in a fork that paid it — so no expectation here
;;; can say "owes 1". The claim below is therefore about the model, which is
;;; what pins the *state* the debt is read from; the owe count itself is
;;; asserted in `ein-infer/tests/obligation_reports.rs`, the in-process sibling
;;; of `stdlib_coverage.rs`. Growing `:expect` a word for an open verdict is a
;;; verdict-vocabulary change and is deliberately not taken here — see
;;; docs/history/m1d_satisfiability/README.md#s1d24--obligations-in-the-saturator.
;;;
;;; Note that `total` is **not** imported: where two rules could reach one
;;; verdict, a fixture separates them by activation (`05_no_room.ein`'s rule).
;;; Here the separation is sharper than usual — the two rules cannot even agree
;;; on what this state is — but the activator is still declared alone, so a
;;; mutant that made `total-owed` behave like `total` has nowhere to hide.

(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed))

(relation is-a  T T         :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation likes Person Food :why "{?1} likes {?2}")

(is-a Ann Person) (is-a Bob Person)
(is-a Soup Food)  (is-a Stew Food)

(total-owed likes is-a)

(likes Bob Stew)

(query
  :goal (likes ?who ?what)
  :no-hypothesis (is-a likes)
  :expect (model (likes Bob Stew)))
